---
/**
 * THE FRONT PAGE FINALLY SHOWS SOMETHING THE MODEL MADE.
 *
 * <ConsentProof /> under the hero shows the PRODUCT: the plugin's panel inside a real Studio, and
 * one request refused then allowed. That answers "is it safe to point at my place". This band
 * answers the question the owner keeps asking instead, which is show me what it builds.
 *
 * Everything else on this page that looks like output is drawn by this site. The four demos in
 * "What it is good at" are honest illustrations of a mechanism — a tree you can read, a critique
 * with three named defects, a Luau panel, a turning wireframe — and not one of them came out of the
 * model. Meanwhile /showcase had been live since 2026-09-20 carrying sixteen interface screens and
 * six maps the deployed model actually built, and the landing neither showed one nor linked to it.
 *
 * NOT ONE NUMBER OR CAVEAT HERE IS WRITTEN BY THIS FILE. Every figure names a field in
 * docs/evidence/ui-showcase/manifest.json — a record the run itself emitted — and
 * tests/built-screen-is-evidence.test.mjs resolves that field and compares. The two caveat
 * sentences are quoted verbatim out of the same manifest. The heading, the small labels and the
 * arrow are the only English on this band that is ours.
 *
 * THE PICTURE IS THE EVIDENCE FILE, BYTE FOR BYTE. public/assets/proof/model-screen-inventory-… is
 * a straight copy of docs/evidence/ui-showcase/screen-inventory--tycoon.svg — the artefact the
 * renderer wrote, not a rasterisation of it and not a crop of one. The guard compares the two files
 * byte for byte. Nothing was cleaned up, and it shows: the cash figure in the header clips against
 * the close button and the item grid fills two rows of a panel built for more. Those stay.
 *
 * WHY IT IS A COMPONENT rather than markup in index.astro — the same reason ConsentProof.astro is:
 * a seventy-line insertion into a file another lane may be holding is how one lane's commit
 * swallows another's. It also means this band carries its own door to /showcase, which matters
 * more than usual here: on the day this shipped the only link to that gallery anywhere in the
 * committed tree was none.
 *
 * NO `data-reveal` ON THE FIGURE ITSELF, only on the head and the body blocks. The reveal pass is
 * held to "the stage is finished before the script arrives" everywhere on this page; an <img> is
 * finished when the bytes land, and hiding it behind a class the script may never add would put
 * the one piece of real output on this page behind the failure mode that
 * tests/reveal-cannot-hide-content.test.mjs exists for.
 */
import { BUILT_SCREEN } from '../data/showcase-proof';

/* Seconds, from the manifest's milliseconds. Derived here rather than written down, so the page
   cannot state a duration the run did not take. One decimal: the run took a minute and a half and
   rounding it to "100 s" would be a different number than the record holds. */
const seconds = (BUILT_SCREEN.ms.value / 1000).toFixed(1);
---

<section class="band" id="built" aria-labelledby="built-title">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="band-head" data-reveal>
      <h2 id="built-title" class="section-title">One screen, as the model wrote it</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">
        Not a mock-up of the product and not a drawing by this page: the model was given the
        construction library the product ships, asked for a tycoon inventory screen, and this is
        what its Luau put on the screen when it was run.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="built-shot">
      {/* NOT lazy, and no `data-reveal`. This is the only picture of output on the page; a reader
          who scrolls to it and meets an empty box because a class never arrived has met the exact
          failure the reveal guard was written for. `width`/`height` are the render's own viewport,
          so the row reserves its box before the file lands. */}
      <img
        src={BUILT_SCREEN.capture.src}
        alt={BUILT_SCREEN.capture.alt}
        width={BUILT_SCREEN.capture.width}
        height={BUILT_SCREEN.capture.height}
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption class="micro built-caption">
        <span>{BUILT_SCREEN.screenId} · {BUILT_SCREEN.capturedLabel}</span>
        <span>{BUILT_SCREEN.hidden.value} {BUILT_SCREEN.hidden.label} — this is the screen the script leaves behind</span>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <ul class="built-figures" role="list" data-reveal>
      {BUILT_SCREEN.figures.map((f) => (
        <li class="built-figure">
          <span class="built-n">{f.value.toLocaleString('en-GB')}</span>
          <span class="micro built-label">{f.label}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    {/* THE DOOR POINTS AT THE RENDER, NOT AT THE GALLERY, AND THAT IS A REPAIR RATHER THAN A CHOICE.
        It linked to {BUILT_SCREEN.gallery} first. That route is real and live — it is an object in
        the worker's D1 static store, published by infra/deploy-showcase.mjs — but it is not an
        Astro page, so tests/links-resolve.test.mjs reports it as a link to a route nothing serves.
        The committed copy of that test does not know about /showcase; a peer's WORKING COPY does,
        with a WORKER_SERVED map and a second test that makes the exemption earn itself, and it was
        uncommitted when this shipped. Editing a file another lane is holding in a shared checkout
        is how one lane's commit swallows another's, so the link came out instead of the guard being
        edited around. docs/backlog/landing-showcase-door.md is the handoff and names the exact two
        lines to put back the moment that exemption is committed.

        The paragraph below still states the counts, because the numbers are the evidence and they
        are resolved from the two manifests either way — what is missing is one anchor, not the
        fact. `scripts/check-site-links.mjs` already carries the same exemption, in a file this lane
        owns, so the route is declared once already. */}
    <p class="built-more" data-reveal>
      <span class="built-took">{seconds} seconds of model time, once.</span>
      <a href={BUILT_SCREEN.capture.src}>This render at full size &rarr;</a>
    </p>

    <p class="built-rest" data-reveal>
      The same run built {BUILT_SCREEN.galleryScreens.value} {BUILT_SCREEN.galleryScreens.label} and
      {BUILT_SCREEN.galleryMaps.value} {BUILT_SCREEN.galleryMaps.label} in all, published at
      <span class="built-route">{BUILT_SCREEN.gallery}</span>, where the failures are shown at the
      same size as the successes.
    </p>

    <p class="micro built-source">
      {BUILT_SCREEN.renderer.text}. {BUILT_SCREEN.library.text} Record: {BUILT_SCREEN.manifest}
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* This band's own rules live here rather than in landing.css for the reason ConsentProof.astro
     gives: that file has been carrying another lane's in-flight work for most of a night, and a
     section is not worth committing somebody else's half-finished stylesheet to ship. Every colour,
     radius and border below is an existing custom property from landing.css — nothing new is
     invented, so the band inherits both themes for free. */

  .built-shot {
    margin: 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r);
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--raised);
  }

  /* The render's own background is a near-black; on the light theme it would otherwise sit on the
     page as an unexplained dark rectangle with a hairline round it. It is a screenshot of a game
     interface, so it is allowed to be dark — the inset shadow is what makes it read as a screen
     rather than as a hole. */
  .built-shot img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  .built-caption {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 4px 18px;
    justify-content: space-between;
    padding: 10px 14px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    color: var(--faint);
  }

  .built-figures {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 16px;
    margin: 24px 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .built-figure {
    padding: 16px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r);
    background: var(--raised);
  }

  .built-n {
    display: block;
    font-size: 26px;
    line-height: 32px;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--ink);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .built-label {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 6px;
    color: var(--faint);
    text-transform: none;
    letter-spacing: 0;
    font-weight: 400;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .built-more {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 6px 18px;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    margin: 18px 0 0;
    font-size: 13.5px;
    line-height: 21px;
  }

  .built-took { color: var(--muted); }

  .built-rest {
    margin: 10px 0 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 13.5px;
    line-height: 21px;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* The route is printed as text rather than linked, for the reason written above it. Monospace so
     a reader can tell it is a path to type and not a phrase. */
  .built-route {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 12.5px;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* `a` is `color: inherit; text-decoration: none` site-wide on the landing, so an unstyled anchor
     here would be invisible as a link. Same four declarations ConsentProof.astro needs. */
  .built-more a {
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: var(--line-strong);
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  .built-more a:hover { text-decoration-color: var(--ink); }

  .built-source {
    margin: 24px 0 0;
    color: var(--faint);
    letter-spacing: 0.6px;
    text-transform: none;
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 12px;
    line-height: 19px;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* 760px is where .proof drops to one column in landing.css; the two bands sit next to each other
     on the page and changing shape at different widths would read as a mistake. Two columns rather
     than one below it, because a single column of four tiles is a column of numbers nobody scans. */
  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .built-figures { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    .built-more { justify-content: flex-start; }
  }
</style>
